<section aria-labelledby="five-h">
  <h2 id="five-h" class="type-h2">The five modes</h2>
  <p>Each mode takes its character from one of the four national traditions in my heritage, Swiss, Norwegian, Swedish and Greek, plus a default. Each has a Light and a Dark version.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="The five modes, scrollable">
    <table class="docs-table type-small">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Mode</th><th scope="col">Code id</th><th scope="col">Tradition</th><th scope="col">Trait</th><th scope="col">In one line</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      {% for m in tokens.modes %}
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><a href="/foundations/color/{{ m.id }}/">{{ m.name }}</a></th>
          <td><code>{{ m.id }}</code></td>
          <td>{{ m.tradition }}</td>
          <td>{{ m.trait }}</td>
          <td>{{ m.summary }}</td>
        </tr>
      {% endfor %}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="status-h">
  <h2 id="status-h" class="type-h2">Canon first</h2>
  <p>Canon is the mode in active use. The other four are explorations: they are kept and checked, but not developed. A mode ships only when there is a named piece of content that needs it.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="changes-h">
  <h2 id="changes-h" class="type-h2">What a mode changes</h2>
  <p>A mode changes only the semantic color roles, such as <code>--arche-surface-brand-primary</code> or <code>--arche-text-brand-primary</code>. Primitives, type, spacing, grid, corner radius, motion and component structure are identical in every mode. Components read roles, never primitives, so they follow the mode without changes.</p>
  <p>Status colors do not change by mode. Alert, Success and Warning mean the same thing everywhere. Every mode passes WCAG AA in Light and Dark.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="use-h">
  <h2 id="use-h" class="type-h2">How to use a mode</h2>
  <p>Two attributes on the <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> element pick the colors. <code>data-mode</code> picks the mode and <code>data-theme</code> picks Light or Dark. With no <code>data-mode</code>, the page uses Canon. With no <code>data-theme</code>, it follows the browser's Light or Dark setting.</p>
<pre class="demo__code" style="padding:16px;overflow-x:auto;background:var(--arche-surface-brand-primary-subtle)" tabindex="0"><code>&lt;html data-mode="omtanke" data-theme="dark"&gt;</code></pre>
  <p>In <code>tokens.css</code>, Canon's values sit on <code>:root</code>. Each other mode has a block that lists only the roles that differ from Canon, with a Dark version after it. Pick the mode with an attribute and the right values apply.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="switch-h">
  <h2 id="switch-h" class="type-h2">Trying a mode on this site</h2>
  <p>Each mode page has a button, "View this page in" followed by the mode name. It sets <code>data-mode</code> on that page only. Nothing is saved, so the next page you open is Canon again. The button is for these docs only. Components never read it.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="accent-h">
  <h2 id="accent-h" class="type-h2">Three accent roles, in plain words</h2>
  <p>Three role names sound alike. They do different jobs.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>--arche-accent-rule</code> is the thin line. It is Canon's end mark, the red rule under the last column of text. Each mode sets its own color for it: Norway Red in Canon, Swiss red in Klarheit, and the mode's blue in the others.</li>
    <li><code>--arche-accent-numeral</code> colors the big numbers in a stat strip. Greece Blue in Canon and Ethos, Swiss red in Klarheit, and each mode's own blue in Nøktern and Omtanke.</li>
    <li><code>--arche-surface-brand-accent</code> is a filled area of accent color, not a line. Only Nøktern's Cross reads it, as the red stripe along the top of the blue band. It holds Norway Red in every mode, but the other modes do not use it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Canon's end mark reads <code>accent-rule</code>, not <code>surface-brand-accent</code>, even though both hold Norway Red there today. They are separate roles so that each can change without moving the other.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="pages-h">
  <h2 id="pages-h" class="type-h2">One page per mode</h2>
  <p>Each mode has a color page with its rules, its main colors, the contrast ratios for the pairs that matter, and the roles that differ from Canon.</p>
  <ul class="cards" role="list">
